        <body>
  <h1>Student Rights & Discipline in District 28: A Parent's Guide</h1>

  <p>District 28, encompassing Forest Hills, Rego Park, Jamaica, Kew Gardens, and Richmond Hill, is committed to fostering a safe and supportive learning environment for all students. This commitment requires a clear understanding of student rights, particularly concerning discipline, and ensuring fair and consistent disciplinary practices in accordance with New York State Education Law and the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA).</p>

  <h2>Understanding Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDRs): A Critical Protection for Students with Disabilities</h2>

  <p>A <b>Manifestation Determination Review (MDR)</b> is a crucial safeguard mandated by IDEA for students with IEPs (Individualized Education Programs) or 504 plans who face disciplinary action, specifically suspension exceeding 10 consecutive school days or a pattern of removals that constitute a change in placement.  This process, governed by 34 CFR § 300.530 and NYS Part 200.2(b)(1)(viii), determines whether the student's behavior that led to the disciplinary action was a direct result of their disability, or if the district failed to properly implement the student's IEP or 504 plan.  In essence, it's a critical review to determine culpability and ensure that students aren't unfairly punished for behaviors stemming from their disability.</p>

  <h3>Step-by-Step Guide to the MDR Process in District 28</h3>

  <ol>
    <li><b>Triggering Event:</b> A suspension exceeding 10 school days (consecutive or cumulative if constituting a pattern) or other disciplinary action that constitutes a change in placement triggers the MDR requirement.  A "change in placement" is often defined as more than 10 cumulative days of suspension if the behaviors are similar, and/or if the length of each suspension is close in time, (e.g. 2 days per week)</li>
    <li><b>Notification:</b>  The school district must notify the parent or guardian of the MDR requirement promptly. This notification must include information about the date, time, and location of the meeting, as well as the purpose of the meeting and the rights of the parents.</li>
    <li><b>IEP Team Review:</b> The IEP team, including the parent(s)/guardian(s), relevant school personnel (teachers, administrators, related service providers), and potentially the student (depending on age and maturity), convenes for the MDR.</li>
    <li><b>Data Collection:</b> The IEP team reviews all relevant information in the student's file, including the IEP/504 plan, teacher observations, any relevant medical or psychological evaluations, and disciplinary records.  The team should also consider whether the student's disability impaired their ability to understand the impact and consequences of the behavior subject to disciplinary action, or impaired the ability of the student to control the behavior subject to disciplinary action.</li>
    <li><b>Determination:</b> The IEP team must answer two key questions:
        <ul>
            <li>Was the conduct in question caused by, or did it have a direct and substantial relationship to, the child's disability?</li>
            <li>Was the conduct in question the direct result of the school district's failure to implement the IEP?</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><b>Outcome:</b>
        <ul>
            <li><b>If the behavior *is* a manifestation of the disability OR the result of IEP implementation failure:</b> The IEP/504 plan must be reviewed and revised to address the behavior. The student generally returns to their placement (unless the parents and the district agree otherwise as part of the IEP process), or an alternative placement is determined through the IEP process that better meets the student’s needs. Further, the school is required to conduct a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA), unless one had been conducted prior to the behavior that resulted in the suspension. The IEP team must then develop a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP) to address the behavior.</li>
            <li><b>If the behavior is *not* a manifestation of the disability AND the IEP was properly implemented:</b> The same disciplinary procedures applied to students without disabilities may be applied to the student with a disability. However, the district must still provide FAPE (Free Appropriate Public Education).</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><b>Parent Rights:</b> Parents have the right to disagree with the MDR outcome and can pursue due process to challenge the decision.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Common District 28 MDR Pitfalls and Advocacy Tips</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Inadequate IEP Implementation:</b> Districts sometimes claim the IEP was properly implemented without sufficient evidence.  Request detailed documentation of all services, accommodations, and modifications provided to your child.  Maintain your own records.</li>
    <li><b>Pre-Determined Outcomes:</b> Watch out for MDR meetings where the outcome seems predetermined.  Advocate for a thorough and unbiased review.</li>
    <li><b>Insufficient Team Participation:</b> Ensure all relevant IEP team members are present and actively participating in the MDR.</li>
    <li><b>Lack of Data:</b> Insist on reviewing all relevant data, including incident reports, teacher observations, and any functional behavioral assessments.</li>
    <li><b>Failure to Consider Mitigating Circumstances:</b> The team must consider any mitigating circumstances that may have contributed to the behavior.</li>
    <li><b>Parent Advocacy Tip:</b> Prepare thoroughly for the MDR meeting. Bring documentation, notes, and any relevant information to support your case. Consider bringing a special education advocate or attorney to the meeting.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Advice Regarding Suspensions in District 28: Know Your Rights</h2>

  <p>If your child is facing suspension, it's crucial to understand your rights under New York State Education Law § 3214 and IDEA.  Suspension, particularly for students with disabilities, can have significant negative impacts on their academic progress and social-emotional well-being.</p>

  <h3>Key Rights and Actions to Take:</h3>

  <ol>
    <li><b>Right to Notification:</b> You have the right to be notified in writing of the reason for the suspension, the duration of the suspension, and the process for appealing the suspension.</li>
    <li><b>Right to a Hearing:</b> For suspensions exceeding five school days, your child has the right to a formal hearing where you can present evidence and witnesses on their behalf. Shorter suspensions may warrant an informal conference.</li>
    <li><b>Documentation is Key:</b> Document everything related to the incident, the school's response, and any communication with school officials. Keep a detailed record of dates, times, names, and what was said.</li>
    <li><b>Request All Documentation:</b> Request all suspension-related documentation from the school, including incident reports, witness statements, and disciplinary records. Under the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA), you have the right to access your child's educational records.</li>
    <li><b>Seek Expert Assistance:</b> Consider seeking assistance from a special education advocate or legal counsel, particularly if your child has an IEP or 504 plan. An advocate can help you understand your rights, navigate the disciplinary process, and advocate for your child's needs.  Organizations like Advocates for Children of New York (AFC) and the Legal Aid Society offer free or low-cost legal services.</li>
    <li><b>Actively Participate:</b> Actively participate in any meetings and hearings related to the suspension. Advocate for your child's educational needs and rights. Don't be afraid to ask questions and challenge the school's decisions.</li>
    <li><b>Alternative Interventions:</b> Remind the school that suspensions should be used as a last resort. Explore alternative interventions, such as counseling, behavior supports, and restorative justice practices.  NYS Part 200.1(jj) emphasizes the use of positive behavioral interventions and supports.</li>
    <li><b>Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA) & Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP):</b> If your child's behavior is interfering with their learning or the learning of others, request a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA) to identify the triggers and functions of the behavior. Based on the FBA, the IEP team should develop a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP) to teach your child appropriate replacement behaviors.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Due Process Rights in District 28: Your Ultimate Recourse</h2>

  <p>If you disagree with the school district's decisions regarding your child's special education needs, including disciplinary actions, you have the right to file a due process complaint under IDEA. Due process is a formal legal process that allows you to resolve disputes with the school district.</p>

  <h3>When to Consider Due Process:</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>The district refuses to conduct an MDR.</li>
    <li>You disagree with the findings of the MDR.</li>
    <li>The district fails to properly implement your child's IEP.</li>
    <li>The district proposes a change in placement that you disagree with.</li>
    <li>You believe the district has violated your child's rights under IDEA.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Steps in the Due Process Process:</h3>

  <ol>
    <li><b>Filing a Complaint:</b> You must file a written due process complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED) and provide a copy to the school district. The complaint must include specific details about the issue in dispute and the relief you are seeking.</li>
     <li><b>Mediation:</b> NYSED offers mediation services to help parents and school districts resolve disputes informally. Mediation is a voluntary process, and both parties must agree to participate.</li>
    <li><b>Resolution Session:</b> Within 15 days of receiving the due process complaint, the school district must convene a resolution session with the parents to try to resolve the dispute.</li>
    <li><b>Due Process Hearing:</b> If the dispute is not resolved through mediation or the resolution session, a due process hearing will be conducted by an impartial hearing officer (IHO) appointed by NYSED.</li>
    <li><b>Hearing Decision:</b> The IHO will issue a written decision based on the evidence presented at the hearing.</li>
    <li><b>Appeals:</b> Either party can appeal the IHO's decision to a State Review Officer (SRO).</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Navigating the Due Process System:</h3>

  <p>The due process system can be complex and time-consuming. It is highly recommended that you seek legal representation from a special education attorney or advocate if you are considering filing a due process complaint.</p>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)</h2>

  <dl>
    <dt><b>Q: What is a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA)?</b></dt>
    <dd><b>A:</b> An FBA is a systematic process of identifying the specific behaviors of concern, the antecedents (triggers) that precede the behaviors, and the consequences that maintain the behaviors. It helps the IEP team understand why a student is engaging in certain behaviors.</dd>

    <dt><b>Q: What is a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP)?</b></dt>
    <dd><b>A:</b> A BIP is a plan developed by the IEP team based on the findings of the FBA. It outlines specific strategies and interventions to address the student's challenging behaviors and teach them appropriate replacement behaviors.</dd>

    <dt><b>Q: Can my child be suspended for behavior related to their disability?</b></dt>
    <dd><b>A:</b> Generally, no. If the behavior is a manifestation of the child's disability, the school district cannot suspend them in the same way they would suspend a student without a disability. The focus should be on revising the IEP/504 plan and providing appropriate supports and interventions.</dd>

    <dt><b>Q: What if I disagree with the school's disciplinary actions?</b></dt>
    <dd><b>A:</b> You have the right to challenge the school's disciplinary actions through informal meetings, formal appeals, and ultimately, due process. Document everything and seek legal advice if needed.</dd>

    <dt><b>Q: What is the difference between an IEP and a 504 plan?</b></dt>
    <dd><b>A:</b> An IEP is a comprehensive plan for students with disabilities who require specialized instruction and related services. A 504 plan provides accommodations and modifications for students with disabilities who do not require specialized instruction but need support to access the general education curriculum.</dd>
  </dl>
</body>
